 % GOAL: state exactly what a hit claims, and name the one class of hits we do not count as a bug, with the reason.
-\noindent\textbf{What we count.} We check each simulated Spectre defense only against the security guarantee its own paper states, with no adversary model: a hit is a run in which the target transmitter meets the defense's transmission criterion inside the violation window, even if no particular attacker could measure it. We do not count one class of hits. ReCon's implementation of STT never marks a floating-point load speculative, so its result is never tainted (\texttt{decode.cc:691--692}); this accounts for about 70\% of the vector version's hits on ReCon's implementation of STT. The skip is a side effect of disabled code that would treat floating-point instructions as possible exceptions, a source of speculation that ReCon's evaluation leaves out~\cite[\S 6.1]{recon}\cutrep{, so we cannot tell whether it is intended, but we note that ReCon as implemented does not taint floating-point loads.}{, so its intent is unclear.}
